AWS Cloud Infrastructure Project 
🎯 Objective

To design and deploy a highly available and scalable web application using core AWS services such as EC2, RDS, EFS, S3, CloudFront, ALB, Auto Scaling, and Route 53.

⚙️ Architecture Overview

Built a multi-tier architecture:

Frontend – Web instances (Nginx) hosted on EC2 in public subnets.

Backend – Database hosted on Amazon RDS (Aurora/MySQL) in private subnets.

Storage – Shared data stored in Amazon EFS and static content in S3.

Networking – Custom VPC with public and private subnets across multiple Availability Zones.

Security – Controlled via Security Groups, IAM Roles, and least-privilege policies.

Performance – Auto Scaling and Load Balancing ensure high availability and fault tolerance.

Global Delivery – CloudFront with Route 53 domain mapping for secure, fast content delivery.

🧱 Step-by-Step Implementation
1. Networking Setup

Created a Custom VPC with two Availability Zones.

Configured Public and Private Subnets.

Attached an Internet Gateway (IGW) for internet access.

Created Route Tables for proper routing between public/private subnets.

2. S3 and CloudFront

Created an S3 bucket to store static website assets (HTML, CSS, JS, images).

Enabled versioning and public read access for static files.

Integrated Amazon CloudFront as CDN for faster global content delivery.

Connected Route 53 domain to CloudFront distribution for custom domain access.

3. EC2 Configuration

Launched Ubuntu EC2 instances in public subnets.

Installed Nginx to serve web pages:

sudo apt update
sudo apt install nginx -y
sudo systemctl enable nginx
sudo systemctl start nginx


Configured index.html and application files.

4. EFS Setup

Created an EFS File System and mount targets in both AZs.

Installed NFS utilities and mounted EFS on EC2 instances:

sudo apt install nfs-common -y
sudo mount -t nfs4 -o nfsvers=4.1 <EFS-DNS>:/ /mnt/efs


Used EFS for shared files like configuration, logs, and plugins.

5. RDS Database Setup

Created RDS (MySQL or Aurora) in private subnets.

Created a Read Replica for high availability.

Connected EC2 web servers to RDS:

mysql -h <RDS-endpoint> -P 3306 -u <username> -p

6. Load Balancing & Auto Scaling

Created an Application Load Balancer (ALB) to distribute incoming traffic.

Configured Target Groups and Health Checks.

Set up an Auto Scaling Group linked with the ALB.

Built a custom AMI from a configured EC2 instance to launch new instances automatically.

7. IAM Configuration

Created IAM Roles for EC2 to access S3, EFS, and CloudWatch.

Applied least privilege policies for security compliance.

8. Route 53 and Domain

Registered a custom domain (e.g., example.online).

Configured CNAME/A Records to route traffic through CloudFront → ALB → EC2.

Issued SSL certificate using AWS ACM for HTTPS support.

🌐 Final Architecture Summary

Users access your domain via Route 53 → CloudFront → ALB.

ALB forwards requests to EC2 web servers.

Web servers fetch shared data from EFS and dynamic data from RDS.

Static content (images, CSS, JS) is served via S3 + CloudFront.

Auto Scaling maintains availability & fault tolerance.

📈 Key AWS Services Used

Compute: EC2, Auto Scaling, AMI

Database: RDS (Aurora/MySQL), Read Replica

Storage: S3, EFS

Networking: VPC, Subnets, Route Tables, IGW

Security: IAM Roles, Security Groups

Load Balancing: Application Load Balancer

CDN & DNS: CloudFront, Route 53

Monitoring: CloudWatch

💡 Outcome

Built a scalable, secure, and cost-optimized web infrastructure.

Gained hands-on experience with AWS architecture, networking, and DevOps deployment.

Learned to handle real-world aspects of availability, automation, and performance optimization.
